<h2 id=definitions>
Definitions</h2>

<dl>
  <dt><dfn export>Archive</dfn> or <dfn export>Uncompressed Archive</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A logical collection of files, directories, and associated metadata.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Barely Compressible</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A condition where the algorithm overhead from compression exceeds the size saving
  so that the normal Compressed Block can't be used.

  <div class=note>
    By definition, this condition varies by the choice of compression algorithm.
    In the case of Zstandard, the minimum overhead is 3 bytes per block of at most 2<sup>21</sup>-1 bytes.
  </div>
  </dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Bit</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The smallest unit of information, representing a binary value of either 0 or 1.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Byte</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A sequence of 8 bits, typically the smallest addressable unit of data.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compress</dfn> or <dfn export>Compression</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The process of transforming an uncompressed archive into a compressed archive, typically to reduce its size.
  This is the inverse operation of decompression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compressor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A component or algorithm responsible for performing compression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Compressed Archive</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A sequence of bytes that represents an archive, organized in a way that is usually, but not necessarily,
  more compact than the naive concatenation of its constituent parts.
  A single uncompressed archive can have multiple valid compressed representations.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Decompress</dfn> or <dfn export>Decompression</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The process of transforming a compressed archive back into its original uncompressed form.
  This is the inverse operation of compression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Decompressor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A component or algorithm responsible for performing decompression.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Dictionary</dfn></dt>
  <dd>TBW</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Directory</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A special type of file that contains references to other files and/or directories,
  forming a hierarchical file system structure.
  It includes its own name and associated metadata.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>File</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A fundamental unit of archival, consisting of a sequence of zero or more content bytes,
  a non-empty byte sequence for its name, and any other associated metadata (e.g., size, modification time, permissions).</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Formula</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A description of a file content as an expression over stored data, modules and resources.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Implicitly-sized</dfn></dt>
  <dd>Being its byte length only available through the context.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Module</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A WebAssembly module that performs the postprocessing part of a transformation.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Postprocess</dfn> or <dfn export>Postprocessor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The act or component that performs operations on data *after* it has been decompressed or transformed,
  but before it is considered fully restored to its original state.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Preprocess</dfn> or <dfn export>Preprocessor</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The act or component that performs operations on data *before* it is compressed or transformed,
  but after it has been retrieved from its original source.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Resource</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A byte sequence shared by multiple invocations of modules, typically a model for the data.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Transform</dfn> or <dfn export>Transformation</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A combined term for matching preprocessing and postprocessing algorithms.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Transformer</dfn></dt>
  <dd>An algorithm responsible for performing transformation.</dd>

  <dt><dfn export>Zstandard block</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The "Block" structure in the Zstandard frame [[RFC8878]], Section 3.1.1.2.</dd>
</dl>

<h2 id=conventions>
Conventions</h2>

<h3 id=expressions>
Expressions</h3>

All expressions are interpreted according to the standard arithmetics, with the following extensions:

*   |a| / |b| equals to floor(|a| ÷ |b|).
*   |a| % |b| is a non-negative modulo of |a| divided by |b|, and equals to |a| - (|a| / |b|) × |b|.
*   |a| &lt;&lt; |b| equals to |a| × 2<sup>|b|</sup>.
*   |a| &gt;&gt; |b| equals to |a| / 2<sup>|b|</sup>.
*   |XX|<sub>16</sub> denotes a hexadecimal integer.
*   (|a|, |b|, …) denotes an ordered array of values.
*   (|XX| |YY| |ZZ|)<sub>16</sub> is a shorthand for (|XX|<sub>16</sub>, |YY|<sub>16</sub>, |ZZ|<sub>16</sub>).
*   |a| || |b| concatenates two arrays |a| and |b|, treating any non-array value as a singleton array (|a|) or (|b|).
*   For array |a|, |a|<sup>|n|</sup> equals to |a| || |a|<sup>|n|-1</sup> when |n| > 0.
    |a|<sup>0</sup> equals to an empty array ().
*   [|a|, |b|] denotes a closed interval between |a| and |b|, both inclusive.
*   [|a|, |b|) denotes a half-open interval between |a| (inclusive) and |b| (exclusive).

All expressions result in an integer between -(2<sup>64</sup> - 1) and 2<sup>64</sup> - 1,
or an array of those integers with the number of elements not exceeding 2<sup>64</sup> - 1.
Note that subexpressions still may exceed this range.

<h3 id=types>
Types</h3>

Types are a set of values and associated encoding scheme.
Unless stated as being [=implicitly-sized=],
all types have the defined byte length out of the context.

<dfn for=type>bool</dfn> is a Boolean value, either true or false.
It has no associated encoding scheme and can't occur in any structure.

<dfn for=type>u8</dfn> is an unsigned 8-bit integer, occupying 1 byte.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>8</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u16le</dfn> is an unsigned 16-bit integer, occupying 2 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order (least significant byte first).
Its range is [0, 2<sup>16</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u32le</dfn> is an unsigned 32-bit integer, occupying 4 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>32</sup>).

<dfn for=type>u64le</dfn> is an unsigned 64-bit integer, occupying 8 bytes,
stored in little-endian byte order.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<strong><dfn for=type>u</dfn>(|n|)</strong>, where |n| is an integer less than 2<sup>64</sup>,
is the smallest integer type that can represent |n| if |n| > 0,
and a zero-sized type if |n| = 0.
In the other words,

* If |n| = 0, u(|n|) is zero-sized.
* If |n| ∈ [1, 2<sup>8</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u8=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>8</sup>, 2<sup>16</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u16le=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>16</sup>, 2<sup>32</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u32le=].
* If |n| ∈ [2<sup>32</sup>, 2<sup>64</sup>), u(|n|) is same as [=u64le=].

<dfn for=type>leb128</dfn> is an unsigned 64-bit integer encoded using the variable number of bytes with the LEB128 encoding.
Each byte, except the last, has its most significant bit (MSB) set to 1, indicating that more bytes follow.
The remaining 7 bits of each byte are used to store the integer's value.
The least significant 7 bits of the first byte are the least significant bits of the integer, and so on.
The last byte has its MSB set to 0.
Its range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<div class=note>
  This allows for multiple encodings for given number for any number smaller than 2<sup>32</sup>.
</div>

<strong>|a|[|n|]</strong>, where |n| is an integer, denotes an ordered array of *a*s.
|n|'s range is [0, 2<sup>64</sup>).

<strong>|a|[*]</strong> denotes an [=implicitly-sized=] unordered array of *a*s with the unspecified quantity.
The number of elements is implicitly determined from the context.
This specification uses two kinds of such contexts:

*   When one or more |a|[*] fields are in the structure with the defined byte length,
    the numbers of elements in those fields are increased as needed to exactly fill the required byte length.
    There MUST be a single set of the numbers of elements that satisfy these conditions.
*   When an |a|[*] field is given with a constraint,
    the field extends until the first instance of |a| which doesn't satisfy the required constraint.

<dfn for=type>string</dfn> is an alias of [=u8=][*] with an additional constraint that
the byte sequence is a valid UTF-8 string. [[RFC3629]]

<h3 id=structure>
Structure</h3>

**Structure** is a logical group of one or more ordered **fields** of possibly differing types concatenated together.
Each field has the following properties:

* The descriptive **name** of the field (e.g. [=block/size=], [=cdata/compression_params=]).
* The data **type** of the field (e.g. [=leb128=], [=u8=][size]), with an optional constraint.
* Optional criteria or **conditions** that dictate the presence, value, or interpretation of the field.

Field names can only coincide when the field's type varies by conditions,
in which case at most one such condition is applicable at any time.
The name can be omitted when the field's name is same as the previous field.

The field type may be <dfn for=type>offset</dfn>,
which is a special zero-sized type interpreted as
the offset to the following byte relative to the unspecified file position (origin).
Offsets are only used to measure the number of bytes within a set of fields.
The syntax and semantics of this specification don't depend on the choice of origin.

The optional constraint does not affect the field's encoding and only narrows down the possible set of values. Constraints can be one of the following:

*   |a| ∈ |X| requires that the value is in the specified set |X|.
*   |a| ∉ |X| requires that the value is not in the specified set |X|.
*   |a| = |x| requires that the value is exactly the given value |x|.
    If |x| is a constant expression, this effectively fixes the byte encoding of this field.
*   |a| ≠ |x| requires that the value is not the given value |x|.

The conditions may be one of the following:

* "<dfn for=cond>Optional</dfn>" indicates that conforming implementations MAY include or exclude given field as needed.
* "<dfn for=cond>If</dfn> …" indicates that the field MUST be included only when the specified condition is true.
* "<dfn for=cond>Depends on</dfn> …" indicates that the field's interpretation varies with the specified set of other fields.

Structures are [=implicitly-sized=] only when its byte length is not available through the context
and at least one [=implicitly-sized=] field exists therein.

<h3 id=constants>
Constants</h3>

This specification defines and uses the following constants, all of which are denoted in UPPERCASE.

<table class=data dfn-for=const>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Value
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>HEADER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>278<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>FOOTER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>98&thinsp;68EB&thinsp;D5FD<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE</dfn>
      <td>15D&thinsp;14FC&thinsp;E5F9<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>HEADER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(F8&nbsp;04&nbsp;03&nbsp;6A&nbsp;D6&nbsp;BE)<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>FOOTER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(FD&nbsp;AA&nbsp;AE&nbsp;DE&nbsp;85&nbsp;12)<sub>16</sub>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn noexport>ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC</dfn>
      <td>(F9&nbsp;CA&nbsp;F2&nbsp;A6&nbsp;D0&nbsp;2A&nbsp;01&nbsp;00)<sub>16</sub>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  The shortest LEB128 encoding of each |XXX_TYPE| is a prefix of each |XXX_MAGIC| byte sequence.
</div>

<h2 id=syntax>
Syntax</h2>

<h3 id=block>
Block</h3>

**Blocks** are a typed byte sequence.
The entirety of an unencrypted archive and the beginning of an encrypted archive consists of either blocks or block-like structures.

Blocks have the following generic structure:

<div dfn-for=block>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>type</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ [=FOOTER_TYPE=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 0
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u16le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 1
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u32le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 2
    <tr>
      <td>
      <td>[=u64le=]
      <td>[=If=] [=block/type=] % 4 = 3
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=block/size=]]
      <td>[=Depends on=] [=block/type=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>block_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>padding</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
      <td>[=If=] ([=block_end=] - [=block_start=]) % 2 = 1
  </tbody>
</table>

Some [=block/type=] values, including [=FOOTER_TYPE=], are also used to denote block-like structures.
For example the archive footer has to have a distinct block type in order to be placed after blocks.
This specification doesn't assign any meaning to such block-like structures unless explicitly stated.

The presence of [=block/padding=] ensures that there are even number of bytes in any block.

The following sections describe the contents of [=block/data=] field according to each defined [=block/type=].
Since [=block/type=] also determines the byte length of [=block/size=],
there are generally multiple [=block/type=] values assigned for each logical block type.

</div>

<h4 id=shared-external-structure>
Shared external structure</h4>

Many block types share the same external structure, only varying its internal structure.
It may or may not have been compressed
and may use different <dfn for=block>compression_method</dfn> or <dfn for=block>checksum_method</dfn>
specified by the parent structure.
Each [=checksum_method=] also has an associated, fixed <dfn for=block>checksum_size</dfn>.

The compressed external structure (<dfn for=block>compressed_data</dfn>) consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=cdata>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size_delta</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compression_params</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>[=Depends on=] [=compression_method=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None, [=depends on=] [=checksum_method=]
  </tbody>
</table>

The compressed size (<dfn>compressed_size</dfn>) is inferred from the block size:
[=cdata/compressed_size=] = [=cdata/data_end=] - [=cdata/data_start=].
The [=cdata/original_size_delta=] field encodes the original size
<dfn>original_size</dfn> = [=cdata/compressed_size=] + [=cdata/original_size_delta=].
Therefore [=cdata/compressed_size=] MUST be equal to or less than [=cdata/original_size=];
any [=barely compressible=] block data MUST NOT use compression.

</div>

The uncompressed external structure (<dfn for=block>uncompressed_data</dfn>) consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=udata>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>data</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>checksum</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=checksum_size=]]
      <td>[=If=] [=checksum_method=] is not None
  </tbody>
</table>

</div>

<h5 id=zstandard-compression-method>
Zstandard compression method</h5>

This specification only defines a single compression method, Zstandard [[RFC8878]].
Its parameters ([=cdata/compression_params=]) are as follows:

<div dfn-for=zstd>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>window_descriptor</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

The meaning of the [=window_descriptor=] field is identical to the Zstandard's `Window_Descriptor` byte [[RFC9659]].

TBW

</div>

Each data compressed by this method is a sequence of Zstandard blocks,
without an additional framing provided by Zstandard frames.
Conforming decompressors MUST process those blocks consecutively until the final block is signalled by the block header,
at which point all compressed bytes should have been consumed.

<h5 id=xxh64-checksum-method>
XXH64 checksum method</h5>

This specification only defines a single checksum method, XXH64 [[XXHASH]].
Its seed MUST be zero and the result should be stored as [=u64le=].
The resulting [=checksum_size=] is 8.

<h4 id=spacer-block>
Spacer block</h4>

Blocks 0<sub>16</sub> through 3<sub>16</sub> are **spacer blocks**.
Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=spacer>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>space</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=block/size=]] = (0)<sup>[=block/size=]</sup>
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

</div>

Spacer blocks can skip any number of bytes besides from a single byte,
which is not required due to the presence of blockwise [=block/padding=].

The skipped bytes MUST be filled with zero bytes in order to avoid data leak.
Conforming compressors are strongly RECOMMENDED to utilize sparse files for performance.

<h4 id=size-hint-block>
Size hint block</h4>

Block 4<sub>16</sub> is a **size hint block**.
Its data consists of the following:

<div dfn-for=szhint>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] ≠ 0
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

If this block is present, it MUST be the first block after the header
and its [=szhint/size_hint=] field SHOULD equal to the footer's [=archive/size_hint=] field.

</div>

<h4 id=metadata-block>
Metadata block</h4>

Blocks 10<sub>16</sub> through 1F<sub>16</sub> are **metadata blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
where each parameter is determined as follows:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th>[=compression_method=]
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>10<sub>16</sub> — 13<sub>16</sub>
      <td>None
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>14<sub>16</sub> — 17<sub>16</sub>
      <td>None
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>18<sub>16</sub> — 1B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>1C<sub>16</sub> — 1F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0001&thinsp;|m||c||ss|</code> where:

    * |m| indicates the compression method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]].
    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<div dfn-for=meta>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>previous_metadata_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_modules</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_resources</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_dictionaries</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_directories</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>num_files</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=meta/entries_end=] - [=meta/entries_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>optional_metadata_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>module_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_modules=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>resource_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_resources=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])[[=meta/num_dictionaries=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_parents</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_directories=])[[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] - 1]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_params</dfn>
      <td>[=meta/file_param=][[=meta/num_files=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_offsets</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/entries_size=])[[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] - 1]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries</dfn>
      <td>[=meta/entry=][[=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=]]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entries_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>optional_metadata</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][[=meta/optional_metadata_size=]]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

All relative offsets are measured from the beginning of the current metadata block,
implying that the offset of zero is invalid.
All valid relative offsets are also even.

Each metadata block describes the [=content blocks=] in its [=segment=],
i.e. blocks appeared after the previous metadata block, if any, and until the current metadata block.
The previous metadata block is linked via [=meta/previous_metadata_offset=]
which is also the maximum possible offsets that can occur in this particular metadata block.
If there is no prior metadata block,
[=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] is the relative offset
to the end of [=archive/header_magic=] in the Unencrypted Archive structure ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
plus 1 so that it never points to a valid block.

[=meta/num_modules=], [=meta/num_resources=] and [=meta/num_dictionaries=] are
the numbers of module, resource and dictionary blocks in the [=segment=] respectively.
Relative offsets to those blocks are
later recorded as [=meta/module_offsets=], [=meta/resource_offsets=] and [=meta/dictionary_offsets=] respectively,
in the order of appearance.

The metadata block represents zero or more files and zero or more directories,
collectively called [=meta/entries=] and numbered together.
The root directory is numbered 0,
subsequent directories are numbered from 1 to [=meta/num_directories=],
and individual files are numbered from ([=meta/num_directories=] + 1) to ([=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=]).
Directories can't form a cycle: a parent of the entry index |k| MUST be less than |k| in [=meta/entry_parents=].
The parent of the root directory (entry 0) and the entry 1 is always fixed to None and the root directory,
so they are omitted.

File parameters (<dfn>file_param</dfn>) occur for each file (but not directory), and consist of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_dictionary</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/num_dictionaries=])
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>file_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=u=]([=meta/previous_metadata_offset=])
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

[=meta/file_dictionary=] is the dictionary used by the block at [=meta/file_offset=]
as a <em>1-based</em> index, or zero if no dictionary is used.
Therefore [=meta/file_dictionary=] = |k| refers to the block at [=meta/dictionary_offsets=][|k| - 1],
and [=meta/num_dictionaries=] is the maximum possible value for it.
A relative offset to the corresponding [=leaf block=] is recorded as [=meta/file_offset=],
which can't be zero.

TODO: An output index when the formula block at [=meta/file_offset=] has multiple outputs.

Entry structure (<dfn>entry</dfn>) then occurs for each directory and file, in this order, and consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_name</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*] ≠ 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>entry_name_sentinel</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] = 0
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>original_size</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] this entry is for a file
  </tbody>
</table>

Each entry is named according to [=meta/entry_name=].
No name can contain a zero byte, and there are two allowed encodings:

* WTF-8 encoding [[WTF-8]], which is an extension to UTF-8 and losslessly encodes a lone surrogate pair in UTF-16; or
* Byte C1<sub>16</sub> followed by the raw byte sequence.

  <div class=note>
    Byte C1<sub>16</sub> was chosen because it can't occur in WTF-8 and any other known extension to UTF-8.
  </div>

Conforming compressors are strongly RECOMMENDED to use the WTF-8 encoding,
and SHOULD only use the raw byte sequence as a round-trip measure.

The [=meta/entries=] array is the only part that has the unspecified number of bytes,
which is therefore available as [=meta/entries_size=] beforehand.
Any other field can be randomly accessed after reading the initial set of [=leb128=] fields.

[=meta/optional_metadata=] is described in Optional metadata ([[#optional-metadata]]).

</div>

<h4 id=content-block>
Content blocks</h4>

Any other block is a <dfn>content block</dfn> which has to be described by a subsequent [[#metadata-block|metadata block]].
A metadata block and all content blocks between the previous metadata block (or the beginning of the archive if none)
and that metadata block form a <dfn>segment</dfn>.
As an exception, an archive without a metadata block ([[#unencrypted-archive]]) forms a single segment with no metadata block.
Each segment MUST be self-contained:
every reference from a block in a segment, whether by an index or by an offset, MUST point to a block in the same segment.

Content blocks are classified as follows:

* A <dfn>leaf block</dfn> represents the content of a file by itself.
    [[#compressed-block|Compressed leaf blocks]], [[#uncompressed-block|uncompressed leaf blocks]]
    and [[#formula-block|formula blocks]] are leaf blocks.
* A <dfn>non-leaf block</dfn> doesn't represent any file by itself,
    and only supplies data referenced by one or more formula blocks.
    [[#compressed-block|Compressed non-leaf blocks]] and [[#uncompressed-block|uncompressed non-leaf blocks]]
    are non-leaf blocks.
* An <dfn>auxiliary block</dfn> supplies data used to decode other blocks.
    [[#dictionary-block|Dictionary blocks]], [[#module-block|module blocks]] and [[#resource-block|resource blocks]]
    are auxiliary blocks.

Dictionary, module and resource blocks are respectively indexed in the order of appearance within the segment.
Two conventions are used for these indices:

* Fields in content blocks use <em>0-based</em> indices,
    because the presence of a reference is signalled separately (e.g. by the block type).
* Fields in the metadata block that may indicate the absence of a reference use <em>1-based</em> indices,
    where zero denotes the absence.

<h5 id=block-order>
Block order</h5>

Blocks in a segment MUST be ordered so that a formula block is resolved by blocks preceding it
and all data required to evaluate it follows it:

* A dictionary block MUST precede any block using it.
* A module or resource block MUST precede any formula block referencing it.
* Every non-leaf block MUST be referenced by at least one formula block,
    and MUST follow all formula blocks referencing it.
* Leaf blocks are otherwise not constrained and MAY be interleaved with non-leaf blocks.

<div class=note>
  These rules make every reference from a formula block to a non-leaf block a forward reference.
  Therefore, when a non-leaf block is read in a streaming fashion,
  all formula blocks referencing it have already been read,
  and the decompressor knows exactly when the block can be discarded.
  Likewise, the decompressor never has to retain a formula block after all non-leaf blocks it references have been read.
</div>

A leaf block and all subsequent content blocks up to the next leaf block or the end of the segment
form a <dfn>parcel</dfn>.
When no leaf block is interleaved with non-leaf blocks,
a parcel consists of a leaf block, the non-leaf blocks it references if it is a formula block,
and possibly auxiliary blocks.

<figure id=fig-segments>
  <pre class=include-raw>path: figures/segments.svg</pre>
  <figcaption>
    An archive with two segments, each ending with the metadata block describing it,
    and four [=parcels=], each starting with a leaf block.
    Arrows denote references from formula blocks to non-leaf blocks (solid) and to a module block (dashed).
    The module block precedes the first leaf block in its segment, so it belongs to no parcel.
    Dictionary, resource and spacer blocks are omitted,
    and no leaf block is interleaved with non-leaf blocks.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h5 id=compressed-block>
Compressed block</h5>

Blocks 20<sub>16</sub> through 3F<sub>16</sub> are **compressed blocks**.
Compressed blocks are parametrized by following variables:

<div dfn-for=cblock>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th><dfn>leaf</dfn>
      <th><dfn>has_dictionary</dfn>
      <th>[=compression_method=]
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>20<sub>16</sub> — 23<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>24<sub>16</sub> — 27<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>28<sub>16</sub> — 2B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>2C<sub>16</sub> — 2F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>30<sub>16</sub> — 33<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>34<sub>16</sub> — 37<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>False
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>38<sub>16</sub> — 3B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>3C<sub>16</sub> — 3F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>True
      <td>[[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]]
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number
  <code>001|n|&thinsp;|d||c||ss|</code> where:

    * |n| indicates the block class: 0 for a leaf block, 1 for a non-leaf block.
    * |d| indicates dictionary usage: 0 for no dictionary, 1 for dictionary present.
    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

Its data consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_index</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=cblock/has_dictionary=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>compressed_data</dfn>
      <td>[=block/compressed_data=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

If a dictionary is in use, its <em>0-based</em> index is specified as [=cblock/dictionary_index=].
This field is intended for linear scanning,
and the metadata block contains all necessary information for random access.
[=cblock/compressed_data=] uses the Shared compressed external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]])
with [=compression_method=] and [=checksum_method=] determined as above.

A compressed block is a single unit of decompression:
reading any part of its content requires decompressing it from the beginning.
Content larger than a desired unit of random access is split into multiple blocks:

* In general, a file is represented by a [[#formula-block|formula block]] concatenating multiple non-leaf blocks.
    The formula block then acts as an index for random access within the file.
* In an archive without a metadata block ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
    the file is simply represented by multiple leaf blocks.

<div class=note>
  A checksum of a non-leaf block is optional and only useful for partial reads.
  The output checksum of a formula block covers its entire output,
  so a decompressor that only reads a part of the output can't verify it without reading the whole output.
  Instead it can verify the checksums of non-leaf blocks it has actually read.
</div>

TODO: Whether [=checksum_method=] covers compressed or original bytes.
The latter is desirable for the integrity of the file content,
and required for verifying partial reads through non-leaf blocks.

</div>

<h5 id=uncompressed-block>
Uncompressed block</h5>

Blocks 40<sub>16</sub> through 4F<sub>16</sub> are **uncompressed blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
where each parameter is determined as follows:

<div dfn-for=ublock>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th><dfn>leaf</dfn>
      <th>[=checksum_method=]
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>40<sub>16</sub> — 43<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>44<sub>16</sub> — 47<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
    <tr>
      <td>48<sub>16</sub> — 4B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>None
    <tr>
      <td>4C<sub>16</sub> — 4F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>[[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]]
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number
  <code>0100&thinsp;|n||c||ss|</code> where:

    * |n| indicates the block class: 0 for a leaf block, 1 for a non-leaf block.
    * |c| indicates the checksum method: 0 for None, 1 for [[#xxh64-checksum-method|XXH64]].
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure of an uncompressed block represents a raw, uninterpreted byte sequence.
This block type MUST be used when given data is barely compressible and can't be represented in terms of compressed blocks.

<div class=note>
  This is mandatory because each Zstandard block incurs 3 bytes of overhead
  and [=cdata/original_size_delta=] can't be negative.
</div>

</div>

<h5 id=dictionary-block>
Dictionary block</h5>

Blocks 50<sub>16</sub> through 53<sub>16</sub> are **dictionary blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=] and None as [=checksum_method=].

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0101&thinsp;00|ss|</code> where:

    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

<div dfn-for=dict>

TBW

</div>

<h5 id=module-block>
Module block</h5>

Blocks 60<sub>16</sub> through 63<sub>16</sub> are **module blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=] and None as [=checksum_method=].

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0110&thinsp;00|ss|</code> where:

    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure after decompression is as follows:

<div dfn-for=module>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>wasm_module</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

TODO: The WebAssembly profile, the module interface and the identification by a hash.

</div>

<h5 id=resource-block>
Resource block</h5>

Blocks 64<sub>16</sub> through 67<sub>16</sub> are **resource blocks**.
Their data uses the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=] and None as [=checksum_method=].

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0110&thinsp;01|ss|</code> where:

    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

The internal structure after decompression is an uninterpreted byte sequence,
which is supplied to modules by formula blocks referencing it.

<div dfn-for=resource>

TODO: How resources are supplied to modules, and the identification by a hash.

</div>

<h5 id=formula-block>
Formula block</h5>

Blocks 70<sub>16</sub> through 7F<sub>16</sub> are **formula blocks**.
Formula blocks are parametrized by following variables:

<div dfn-for=formula>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Block Types
      <th><dfn>has_dictionary</dfn>
      <th><dfn>has_output_checksum</dfn>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>70<sub>16</sub> — 73<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>False
    <tr>
      <td>74<sub>16</sub> — 77<sub>16</sub>
      <td>False
      <td>True
    <tr>
      <td>78<sub>16</sub> — 7B<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>False
    <tr>
      <td>7C<sub>16</sub> — 7F<sub>16</sub>
      <td>True
      <td>True
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class=note>
  Alternatively, the block type can be interpreted as a binary number <code>0111&thinsp;|d||c||ss|</code> where:

    * |d| indicates dictionary usage: 0 for no dictionary, 1 for dictionary present.
    * |c| indicates the presence of output checksums: 0 for absent, 1 for present.
    * |ss| indicates the size of the size field: 0 for [=u8=], 1 for [=u16le=], 2 for [=u32le=], 3 for [=u64le=].
</div>

Its data consists of the following:

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>dictionary_index</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>[=If=] [=formula/has_dictionary=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>formula_data</dfn>
      <td>[=block/compressed_data=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

If a dictionary is in use, its <em>0-based</em> index is specified as [=formula/dictionary_index=], as in compressed blocks.
[=formula/formula_data=] uses the Shared compressed external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]])
with [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]] as [=compression_method=] and None as [=checksum_method=].

<div class=note>
  Formula blocks always use Zstandard, which can still store data verbatim with raw Zstandard blocks.
  Many small formula blocks are expected to share a dictionary to compensate for the lack of shared compression context.
</div>

A formula block describes the content of a file as an expression over non-leaf blocks following it,
evaluated with modules and resources preceding it.
The internal structure of [=formula/formula_data=] is described in Formula data ([[#formula-data]]).

</div>

<h4 id=reserved-blocks>
Reserved blocks</h4>

Blocks whose type's hexadecimal representation is at least 2 digits long
and starts with D<sub>16</sub> (e.g. D0<sub>16</sub> through DF<sub>16</sub>, D00<sub>16</sub> through DFF<sub>16</sub>, …)
are <dfn>reserved for development environments</dfn>.
This specification doesn't assign any meanings to these blocks.
These blocks MUST be written or read only with specific instructions to enable them.

Blocks which type's hexadecimal representation is at least 2 digits long
and starts with E<sub>16</sub> (e.g. E0<sub>16</sub> through EF<sub>16</sub>, E00<sub>16</sub> through EFF<sub>16</sub>, …)
are <dfn>reserved for experiments</dfn>.
This specification doesn't assign any meanings to those blocks.

The following block types are invalid due to the use as block-like structures.
The valid archive MUST NOT include such block types as a proper block.

* Block 278<sub>16</sub> ([=const/HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the unencrypted archive header.
* Block 98&thinsp;68EB&thinsp;D5FD<sub>16</sub> ([=const/FOOTER_TYPE=]) is used for the archive footer.
* Block 15D&thinsp;14FC&thinsp;E5F9<sub>16</sub> ([=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=]) is used for the encrypted archive header.

<h3 id=archive>
Archive</h3>

<h4 id=unencrypted-archive>
Unencrypted Archive</h4>

An **unencrypted archive** consists of a header, one or more blocks and a footer.

<div dfn-for=archive>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>header_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][6] = [=const/HEADER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint_block</dfn>
      <td>[[#size-hint-block|size_hint_block]]
      <td>[=cond/Optional=]
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>blocks</dfn>
      <td>[[#block|block]][*]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>archive_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][6] = [=const/FOOTER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_size</dfn>
      <td>[=u16le=] = [=archive/footer_end=] - [=archive/footer_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_start</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>size_hint</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=] = [=archive/archive_end=] - [=archive/archive_start=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>metadata_backward_offset</dfn>
      <td>[=leb128=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_end</dfn>
      <td>[=offset=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>footer_size_copy</dfn>
      <td>[=u16le=] = [=archive/footer_size=]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

[=archive/header_magic=] is a fixed 6-byte sequence to identify the file as an unencrypted Aqueous archive.
This header also marks the beginning of the archive, when the archive doesn't start at the beginning of the file.

[=archive/footer_magic=] is a fixed 6-byte sequence to mark the beginning of the footer.
The footer also marks the end of the archive, when the archive doesn't end at the end of the file.
The offset between [=archive/header_magic=] and [=archive/footer_magic=] is recorded as [=archive/size_hint=],
which MAY be also replicated as [=archive/size_hint_block=] if random access is desired.

The footer size, which is the size of all fields after (but not including) [=archive/footer_magic=],
is recorded both in [=archive/footer_size=] and in [=archive/footer_size_copy=].
Implementations are therefore expected to first identify [=archive/header_magic=] and skip to the end of the file
to read [=archive/footer_size_copy=] and eventually [=archive/footer_magic=] to read the footer.

<div class=note>
  [=archive/footer_size=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=] are [=u16le=] instead of [=leb128=].
  Therefore the footer size is limited to 65,535 bytes,
  and a single read of 65,543 bytes (including [=archive/footer_magic=] and [=archive/footer_size_copy=]) is
  sufficient to guarantee that the entire footer is read.
</div>

[=archive/metadata_backward_offset=] is the offset from [=archive/footer_start=] to the beginning of last metadata block,
just like [=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] is the offset from the beginning of the current metadata block to the previous one.

[=archive/blocks=] are logically divided into multiple parts,
consisting of one or more [=content blocks=] and a single [[#metadata-block|metadata block]] describing them,
with optional spacer blocks in between.
Each part can be independently processed and decompressed in the reading order.
There SHOULD NOT be multiple [=meta/entry|entries=] with the same path across metadata blocks,
and if there are, only the last one is considered valid.

Alternatively, [=archive/blocks=] can have no metadata block.
Such archive decompresses into a single unnamed file,
whose content is the concatenation of the contents of all [=leaf blocks=] in the order of appearance.
It decompresses into an empty file if there is no leaf block.

<div class=note>
  This allows a single file of unknown size to be compressed in a streaming fashion,
  by writing each unit of random access as a separate leaf block as soon as it is compressed.
  It is analogous to a gzip file that doesn't need to contain the name of the decompressed file.
  Compressing multiple files in a streaming fashion is not supported:
  a file spanning multiple non-leaf blocks requires a formula block preceding them,
  which can only be written after all of them have been compressed.
</div>

</div>

<h4 id=encrypted-archive>
Encrypted Archive</h4>

An archive might have been **encrypted**,
in which case it consists of a single block of type [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_TYPE=] followed by an encrypted binary payload.

<div dfn-for=envelope>

<table class=data>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name
      <th>Type
      <th>Conditions
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>encrypted_header_magic</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][8] = [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>lg_work_factor</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=] ∈ [0C<sub>16</sub>, 3F<sub>16</sub>)
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>scrypt_ephemeral_secret</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][16]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>encrypted_file_key</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>age_header_mac</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][32]
      <td>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn>payload</dfn>
      <td>[=u8=][*]
      <td>
  </tbody>
</table>

All fields except for [=payload=] are used to reconstruct an AGE v1 textual file header [[AGE]] as follows:

<pre>
age-encryption.org/v1
-> scrypt <var>scrypt_ephemeral_secret_base64</var> <var>lg_work_factor_decimal</var>
<var>encrypted_file_key_base64</var>
--- <var>age_header_mac_base64</var>
</pre>

Where,

* |scrypt_ephemeral_secret_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/scrypt_ephemeral_secret=] without padding ([[RFC4648]], Section 4);
* |lg_work_factor_decimal| is the decimal representation of [=envelope/lg_work_factor=] without leading zeros;
* |encrypted_file_key_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/encrypted_file_key=] without padding;
* |age_header_mac_base64| is the base64 encoding of [=envelope/age_header_mac=] without padding.

Subsequent [=payload=] contains the file nonce and encrypted chunks and interpreted in accordance to the AGE v1 format.

The encrypted payload MUST decrypt into a valid unencrypted archive ([[#unencrypted-archive]]),
with the following additional constraints:

* [=archive/size_hint_block=] is no longer optional;
* No spacer block ([[#spacer-block]]) is allowed, in order to discourage an in-place modification of the encrypted archive;
* No trailing bytes are allowed after the archive.

</div>

<h3 id=optional-metadata>
Optional metadata</h3>

The [=meta/optional_metadata=] field of the metadata block ([[#metadata-block]]) holds the **optional metadata**, encoded in CBOR [[RFC8949]].
This part encodes any metadata that is not strictly necessary to read the file,
including associated dates and times, access control information and comments.

The optional metadata is described by the following CDDL [[RFC8610]] definitions,
where `etime` is the CDDL type name for the extended time defined in [[!RFC9581]]:

<pre class=cddl>
aqr-opt-metadata = { $$aqr-opt-metadata }

aqr-offset = uint .gt 0

aqr-text =
  tstr /          ; Unicode
  #6.273(bstr) /  ; UTF-16 + surrogate compat
  bstr            ; In unknown encoding

aqr-time =
  $aqr-time /               ; UTC timestamp or its scaling
  [$aqr-time] /             ; In unknown time zone, encoded like UTC (*)
  [$aqr-time, offset: int]  ; In a known time zone, offset from UTC in 15 minutes ($)

$aqr-time /= #6.100(int)  ; 1d (FAT atime*)
$aqr-time /= #6.121(int)  ; 2s (FAT mtime*, ZIP*, CAB*)
$aqr-time /= int          ; 1s (~ext3, HFS/HFS+, tar)
$aqr-time /= #6.122(int)  ; 10ms (FAT ctime*, ISO 9660$, exFAT$)
$aqr-time /= #6.123(int)  ; 1ms
$aqr-time /= #6.124(int)  ; 1us (UDF)
$aqr-time /= #6.125(int)  ; 100ns (NTFS, RAR*, 7z)
$aqr-time /= #6.126(int)  ; 1ns (ext4, ZFS, Btrfs, APFS)
$aqr-time /= etime

$$aqr-opt-metadata //= (
  ? &(archive-comment: -1) => aqr-text
  &(entry-extras: -2) => [* aqr-entry-extra]
)

aqr-entry-extra = (
  ? &(crtime: -1) => aqr-time
  ? &(ctime: -2) => aqr-time
  ? &(mtime: -3) => aqr-time
  ? &(atime: -4) => aqr-time
  ? &(arctime: -5) => aqr-time

  ? &(comment: -6) => aqr-text

  ? &(unix-mode: -70) => uint
  ? &(unix-uid: -71) => uint
  ? &(unix-gid: -72) => uint

  ; TODO:
  ; NTFS attributes (ZIP extra 0x000a)
  ; UNIX attributes (ZIP extra 0x000d) - symlink target
  ; other extras for round trip

  ? &(zip-extras: -100) => aqr-zip-extras
)

aqr-zip-extras = { * aqr-zip-extra-id => bstr }
;aqr-zip-extra-id = 0..0xffff
; TODO: should exclude or at least discourage the following:
;       0x0001 - ZIP64
;       0x5455 - extended timestamp
;       0x7075 - unicode path (info-zip)
;       0x6375 - unicode comment (info-zip)
</pre>

There MUST NOT be any following bytes after this CBOR item.

Issue: The precisions of `$aqr-time` do not cover every precision found in the wild
(e.g. minutes in ASN.1 `UTCTime`, 2<sup>-32</sup> s in NTP, 1/300 s in SQL Server `DATETIME`),
and its time zone offset cannot express minute offsets as in UDF.
The extended time (`#6.1001`) [[RFC9581]] would be the desirable representation,
but it is too verbose for every timestamp and has a semantic gap:
its uncertainty (key -7) and guarantee (key -8) are symmetric and about measurement,
while a truncated or rounded timestamp only bounds the true time to one side.
This would need a new elective key (tentatively -14, a quantization interval)
meaning that the true time lies in a given interval relative to the base time.
`#6.121`..`#6.127` would then be defined as shortcuts for commonly used `#6.1001` values.
Since editing an archive can mix multiple precisions in a single file,
the precision should ideally be recorded per timestamp as above, not per archive.

TBW

<h3 id=formula-data>
Formula data</h3>

This section describes the internal structure of [=formula/formula_data=] in formula blocks ([[#formula-block]]),
after it has been decompressed.

TODO: The internal structure is expected to include:

* The output size;
* The output checksum, [=If=] [=formula/has_output_checksum=];
* Nodes of the expression: a byte range of non-leaf blocks, a concatenation of nodes,
    and an application of a module to nodes with resources and parameters;
* References to non-leaf blocks, each as an ordinal among the subsequent non-leaf blocks
    and a relative offset to the referenced block.
    The ordinal serves streaming decompressors and the offset serves random access,
    so they MUST agree with each other, and a decompressor detecting a mismatch MUST treat it as an error.
    Offsets are expected to be encoded to compress well, e.g. relative to the end of the previously referenced block;
* References to module and resource blocks, as indices within the segment;
* Resource declarations for each application (memory limit, input and output window sizes, capacity of shared edges);
* Multiple outputs from a single formula block, each of which corresponds to a file;
* References to other files, or their replacement;
* Padding to keep [=formula/formula_data=] compressible, since its original size can't be smaller than the compressed size.

The structure of [=formula/formula_data=] is not randomly accessible,
and a decompressor is expected to read and decompress it in its entirety before evaluating any part of the formula.
Together with the size declared by each node and the offsets of referenced non-leaf blocks,
it then serves as an index for random access within the output,
in the same way as a seek table of other formats.

<div class=note>
  The index grows linearly with the number of non-leaf blocks referenced,
  but it compresses well because most nodes share the same shape and sizes.
  The decompression of the whole [=formula/formula_data=] is a one-time cost per file.
</div>

<h2 id=semantics>
Semantics</h2>

This section defines the meaning of an archive as a function from a byte sequence to an [=archive value=].
Every algorithm here is declarative:
it defines *what* the result is, not how or in which order it has to be computed,
and a conforming implementation MAY compute it in any other way that produces the same result.

The result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=] is either an [=archive value=] or a failure,
which is one of the following:

* An <dfn>invalid archive</dfn> failure occurs when the input violates any requirement of [[#syntax]] or this section.
    It is a property of the input alone, and all conforming decompressors agree on it.
* A <dfn>resource failure</dfn> occurs when the evaluation exceeds a declared or implementation-defined limit.
    It is not a property of the input, and conforming decompressors MAY disagree on it.

A conforming compressor, given an [=archive value=],
MUST produce a byte sequence whose interpretation is that [=archive value=].
This specification doesn't constrain how the compressor chooses blocks, block sizes, dictionaries or formulas.

<h3 id=archive-model>
Archive model</h3>

An <dfn>archive value</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="archive value">root</dfn>, either a [=directory node=] or a [=file node=].
    The latter represents a single unnamed file.
* <dfn for="archive value">extra</dfn>, an [=ordered map=] from integers to CBOR data items [[RFC8949]]
    representing the archive-wide part of the [[#optional-metadata|optional metadata]].

A <dfn>file node</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="file node">content</dfn>, a [=byte sequence=].
* <dfn for="file node">extra</dfn>, a CBOR data item matching `aqr-entry-extra`, or null.

A <dfn>directory node</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="directory node">children</dfn>, an [=ordered map=] from [=entry names=] to [=file nodes=] or [=directory nodes=].
* <dfn for="directory node">extra</dfn>, a CBOR data item matching `aqr-entry-extra`, or null.

An <dfn>entry name</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

* <dfn for="entry name">encoding</dfn>, either "`wtf-8`" or "`raw`".
* <dfn for="entry name">bytes</dfn>, a non-empty [=byte sequence=]
    which is a valid WTF-8 [[WTF-8]] string if [=entry name/encoding=] is "`wtf-8`".

Two [=entry names=] are equal if and only if both items are equal.
In particular, a "`wtf-8`" name and a "`raw`" name are never equal even when their [=entry name/bytes=] are.

No [=entry name/bytes=] of an [=entry name=] can contain 00<sub>16</sub> or 2F<sub>16</sub> (`/`),
or be equal to (2E)<sub>16</sub> (`.`) or (2E 2E)<sub>16</sub> (`..`).

<div class=note>
  These restrictions make every path in an [=archive value=] a plain sequence of components,
  so that no name can refer to the current, parent or nested directory on any common file system.
  Platform-specific restrictions are handled at the extraction time ([[#extraction]]).
</div>

To <dfn>decode an entry name</dfn> from a [=meta/entry_name=] field |bytes|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. If |bytes|[0] is C1<sub>16</sub>, let |name| be an [=entry name=]
        whose [=entry name/encoding=] is "`raw`" and [=entry name/bytes=] are |bytes| without the first byte.
    2. Otherwise, if |bytes| is a valid WTF-8 string, let |name| be an [=entry name=]
        whose [=entry name/encoding=] is "`wtf-8`" and [=entry name/bytes=] are |bytes|.
    3. Otherwise, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
    4. If |name| violates any restriction of an [=entry name=] above, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
    5. Return |name|.
</ol>

<h3 id=interpretation>
Interpreting an archive</h3>

To <dfn export>interpret an archive</dfn> given a [=byte sequence=] |input|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. If |input| starts with [=const/ENCRYPTED_HEADER_MAGIC=]:
        1. TODO: Decrypt the [=envelope/payload=], check the additional constraints in [[#encrypted-archive]],
            and interpret the decrypted archive instead.
    2. Parse |input| as the Unencrypted Archive structure ([[#unencrypted-archive]]) in its entirety.
        If it fails to parse, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
    3. If [=archive/blocks=] contain no [[#metadata-block|metadata block]]:
        1. Let |content| be the concatenation of the [=file contents=] of all [=leaf blocks=] in [=archive/blocks=],
            in the order of appearance.
        2. Return an [=archive value=] whose [=archive value/root=] is a [=file node=]
            with |content| as [=file node/content=] and null as [=file node/extra=],
            and whose [=archive value/extra=] is an empty map.
    4. Let |root| be a [=directory node=] with an empty map as [=directory node/children=] and null as [=directory node/extra=].
    5. Let |extra| be an empty map.
    6. For each [=segment=] |segment| in [=archive/blocks=], in the order of appearance:
        1. Let |segment root| be the result of [=interpret a segment|interpreting=] |segment|.
        2. [=Merge directory nodes|Merge=] |segment root| into |root|.
        3. For each key |k| and value |v| in the top-level map of the segment's [[#optional-metadata|optional metadata]]
            other than `entry-extras`, set |extra|[|k|] to |v|.
    7. Return an [=archive value=] with |root| as [=archive value/root=] and |extra| as [=archive value/extra=].
</ol>

The input to this algorithm is exactly the archive, from the first byte of [=archive/header_magic=]
to the last byte of [=archive/footer_size_copy=].

Issue: An algorithm to locate the archive within a larger file with leading or trailing bytes.

Since every [=content block=] has to be described by a subsequent metadata block ([[#content-block]]),
an archive with a metadata block can't have any content block after the last metadata block.
[=meta/previous_metadata_offset=] and [=archive/metadata_backward_offset=] MUST point to
the metadata blocks found by parsing [=archive/blocks=] as described in [[#metadata-block]] and [[#unencrypted-archive]],
so that a decompressor can find each [=segment=] either by scanning blocks forward or by following offsets backward.

Issue: [=archive/metadata_backward_offset=] is not yet defined when there is no metadata block.

<h4 id=segment-interpretation>
Interpreting a segment</h4>

To <dfn>interpret a segment</dfn> |segment| whose metadata block is |metadata block|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. Let |meta| be the [=decoded data=] of |metadata block|, parsed as its internal structure ([[#metadata-block]]).
    2. Let |d| be [=meta/num_directories=] and |n| be [=meta/num_directories=] + [=meta/num_files=] of |meta|.
    3. Let |nodes| be a [=list=] containing a single [=directory node=]
        with an empty map as [=directory node/children=] and the [=entry extra=] for the entry 0 as [=directory node/extra=].
    4. For each |k| in [1, |n|]:
        1. Let |entry| be |meta|.[=meta/entries=][|k| - 1].
        2. Let |name| be the result of [=decode an entry name|decoding=] |entry|.[=meta/entry_name=].
        3. Let |parent| be 0 if |k| = 1, or |meta|.[=meta/entry_parents=][|k| - 2] otherwise.
        4. If |k| ≤ |d|, let |node| be a [=directory node=] with an empty map as [=directory node/children=].
        5. Otherwise:
            1. Let |param| be |meta|.[=meta/file_params=][|k| - |d| - 1].
            2. Let |block| be the block at the relative offset |param|.[=meta/file_offset=] from |metadata block|.
                It MUST be a [=leaf block=] in |segment|.
            3. If |param|.[=meta/file_dictionary=] is zero, |block| MUST NOT use a dictionary.
                Otherwise, |block| MUST use the dictionary block at |meta|.[=meta/dictionary_offsets=][|param|.[=meta/file_dictionary=] - 1].
            4. Let |content| be the [=file content=] of |block|.
                Its length MUST be |entry|.[=meta/original_size=].
            5. Let |node| be a [=file node=] with |content| as [=file node/content=].
        6. Set the extra of |node| to the [=entry extra=] for the entry |k|.
        7. Let |children| be the [=directory node/children=] of |nodes|[|parent|].
            |children|[|name|] MUST NOT [=map/exist=].
        8. Set |children|[|name|] to |node|.
        9. [=list/Append=] |node| to |nodes|.
    5. Return |nodes|[0].
</ol>

Since every |parent| is less than |k| and at most |d| by [[#metadata-block]], |nodes|[|parent|] is always a [=directory node=] already in |nodes|.

The <dfn>entry extra</dfn> for the entry |k| is the |k|-th element of `entry-extras` in the segment's optional metadata,
or null if there is no such element.

Issue: Whether `entry-extras` includes the root directory, and whether `aqr-entry-extra` has to be a map rather than a group.

<h4 id=segment-merging>
Merging segments</h4>

To <dfn>merge directory nodes</dfn> |later| into |earlier|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. For each |name| → |node| of |later|.[=directory node/children=]:
        1. Let |existing| be |earlier|.[=directory node/children=][|name|], or null if it doesn't [=map/exist=].
        2. If both |existing| and |node| are [=directory nodes=]:
            1. If |node|.[=directory node/extra=] is not null, set |existing|.[=directory node/extra=] to it.
            2. [=Merge directory nodes|Merge=] |node| into |existing|.
        3. Otherwise, set |earlier|.[=directory node/children=][|name|] to |node|.
    2. If |later|.[=directory node/extra=] is not null, set |earlier|.[=directory node/extra=] to it.
</ol>

As a result, a directory appearing in multiple segments has the union of their children,
while a file or a directory replaced by a node of the different kind is replaced as a whole with its descendants.
An entry can't be removed by a later segment.

<div class=note>
  Directories necessarily reappear in later segments to hold new files under them.
  The rule in [[#unencrypted-archive]] that there SHOULD NOT be multiple entries with the same path
  therefore only concerns files.
</div>

Issue: Whether a directory without an [=entry extra=] in a later segment should clear the existing [=directory node/extra=].

<h3 id=block-contents>
Block contents</h3>

The <dfn>decoded data</dfn> of a block using the Shared external structure ([[#shared-external-structure]]),
given its [=compression_method=] and [=checksum_method=], is determined as follows:

* If [=compression_method=] is None, it is [=udata/data=].
* If [=compression_method=] is [[#zstandard-compression-method|Zstandard]],
    it is the result of decompressing [=cdata/data=] as described there, with [=cdata/compression_params=]
    and the [=decoded data=] of the dictionary block in use, if any.
    The result MUST have exactly [=cdata/original_size=] bytes.
* If [=checksum_method=] is not None, the checksum MUST be equal to the checksum computed by the [=checksum_method=].

Issue: Which bytes the checksum covers; see the TODO in [[#compressed-block]].

Issue: The use of the decoded data of a dictionary block (TBW in [[#dictionary-block]]).

The <dfn>stored content</dfn> of a [[#compressed-block|compressed block]] or an [[#uncompressed-block|uncompressed block]],
whether a [=leaf block=] or not, is the [=decoded data=] of its [=cblock/compressed_data=] or its data respectively.
A compressed block using a dictionary uses the dictionary block at [=cblock/dictionary_index=] within the [=segment=].

The <dfn>file content</dfn> of a [=leaf block=] is:

* Its [=stored content=], if it is a compressed or uncompressed block;
* The result of [=evaluate a formula block|evaluating=] it, if it is a [[#formula-block|formula block]].

<h3 id=formula-evaluation>
Formula evaluation</h3>

Issue: This section depends on the internal structure of [=formula/formula_data=], which is TODO in [[#formula-data]].
It currently describes the nodes abstractly.

A <dfn>formula node</dfn> is one of the following:

* A <dfn>slice node</dfn> with a non-leaf block |block|, an offset |o| and a length |l|.
* A <dfn>concatenation node</dfn> with a [=list=] of [=formula nodes=].
* An <dfn>application node</dfn> with a [[#module-block|module block]],
    a [=list=] of [[#resource-block|resource blocks]], a parameter [=byte sequence=],
    a [=list=] of [=formula nodes=] as inputs, a declared output size and resource declarations.
* A <dfn>reference node</dfn> with a reference to another file.

To <dfn>evaluate a formula node</dfn> |node|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. If |node| is a [=slice node=] with |block|, |o| and |l|:
        1. Let |c| be the [=stored content=] of |block|.
        2. |o| + |l| MUST be at most the length of |c|.
        3. Return the bytes of |c| in [|o|, |o| + |l|).
    2. If |node| is a [=concatenation node=], return the concatenation of the results of
        [=evaluate a formula node|evaluating=] each node in order.
    3. If |node| is an [=application node=]:
        1. Let |inputs| be the results of [=evaluate a formula node|evaluating=] each input node in order.
        2. Return the result of [=apply a module|applying=] the module with |inputs|
            and the other items of |node|.
    4. If |node| is a [=reference node=], TODO.
</ol>

To <dfn>evaluate a formula block</dfn> |block|:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. Let |data| be the [=decoded data=] of [=formula/formula_data=] of |block|, parsed as its internal structure.
    2. Let |output| be the result of [=evaluate a formula node|evaluating=] the root [=formula node=] of |data|.
    3. The length of |output| MUST be equal to the output size declared in |data|.
    4. If [=formula/has_output_checksum=] is true, the XXH64 checksum of |output| MUST be equal to the declared one.
    5. Return |output|.
</ol>

<div class=note>
  Inputs are defined as fully evaluated byte sequences.
  Lazy, streaming or partial evaluation is an implementation strategy and doesn't change the result
  ([[#partial-access]]).
</div>

Issue: [=Reference nodes=] (in particular when the referenced file is replaced in a later segment), and multiple outputs.

<h3 id=module-execution>
Module execution</h3>

Issue: This section is tentative and follows `xform-design.md` §5.

A module is a WebAssembly module [[WASM-CORE-2]] stored as [=module/wasm_module=],
instantiated and invoked through the embedding interface defined in the Appendix of [[WASM-CORE-2]].
It MUST conform to the following profile, or an [=invalid archive=] failure occurs:

* WebAssembly 2.0 core with the fixed-width SIMD, executed under the deterministic profile;
    every NaN result is a canonical NaN.
* Multiple memories are allowed.
* Threads, relaxed SIMD, garbage collection, exception handling, memory64 for the working memory
    and imported tables are not allowed.

Issue: The import module name, the exported entry point, how resources and parameters are supplied, and whether memory64 is allowed for input and output memories.

To <dfn>apply a module</dfn> |module| given a [=list=] of input [=byte sequences=] |inputs|,
resources, parameters, a declared output size |size| and resource declarations:

<ol class=algorithm>
    1. For each index |k| of |inputs|, let |W|<sub>|k|</sub> be the declared input window size of |k|-th input,
        and let the <dfn>visible range</dfn> of the |k|-th input be [0, 0).
    2. Let |W|<sub>out</sub> be the declared output window size and |output| be an empty [=byte sequence=].
    3. Instantiate |module| with the host functions below,
        binding the memory |k| + 1 to a zero-filled memory of |W|<sub>|k|</sub> bytes for each input |k|
        and the output memory to a zero-filled memory of |W|<sub>out</sub> bytes.
    4. Invoke the entry point.
        If it traps or any host function below signals an error, return an [=invalid archive=] failure.
        If it exceeds any resource declaration or implementation-defined limit, return a [=resource failure=].
    5. The length of |output| MUST be |size|.
    6. Return |output|.
</ol>

Within the declared memory limit, `memory.grow` MUST succeed.

Host functions are defined as follows, where |lo|<sub>|k|</sub> and |hi|<sub>|k|</sub> denote the [=visible range=] of the |k|-th input:

: <dfn>request</dfn>(|k|, |hi|)
:: |hi| MUST be in [|hi|<sub>|k|</sub>, length of |inputs|[|k|]], and |hi| - |lo|<sub>|k|</sub> MUST be at most |W|<sub>|k|</sub>.
    For each offset |o| in [|hi|<sub>|k|</sub>, |hi|), write |inputs|[|k|][|o|] to the memory |k| + 1 at |o| % |W|<sub>|k|</sub>.
    Then set |hi|<sub>|k|</sub> to |hi|.
: <dfn>release</dfn>(|k|, |lo|)
:: |lo| MUST be in [|lo|<sub>|k|</sub>, |hi|<sub>|k|</sub>]. Set |lo|<sub>|k|</sub> to |lo|.
: <dfn>commit</dfn>(|hi|)
:: |hi| MUST be in [length of |output|, |size|], and |hi| - length of |output| MUST be at most |W|<sub>out</sub>.
    For each offset |o| in [length of |output|, |hi|), append the byte at |o| % |W|<sub>out</sub> of the output memory to |output|.
: <dfn>heartbeat</dfn>()
:: Does nothing.

A violation of any MUST in host functions is signalled as an error to the module invocation.

<div class=note>
  The host writes input memories only within [=request=] and reads the output memory only within [=commit=],
  so the content of every memory depends only on the order of calls made by the module.
  Implementations MAY block in [=request=] until the requested bytes are available
  and in [=commit=] until consumers catch up, or alias memories between modules,
  as long as the result is indistinguishable from this definition.
</div>

Issue: Deterministic fork-join (`spawn`) and channels between tasks.

<h3 id=partial-access>
Partial access</h3>

A decompressor MAY compute only a part of the result of [=interpret an archive|interpreting an archive=],
such as a subset of entries, a byte range of a [=file node/content=], or entry names without contents.
Such a partial result MUST be equal to the corresponding part of the full result, if the full result is an [=archive value=].

A decompressor computing a partial result is not required to detect an [=invalid archive=] failure
caused only by parts it doesn't compute.
It SHOULD verify every checksum covering only the bytes it has computed.

<div class=note>
  For example, a decompressor reading a byte range of a file defined by a formula block can't verify its output checksum,
  but can verify the checksums of non-leaf blocks it has read.
  A decompressor looking up a single path has to consider every [=segment=] that may replace it,
  which is possible by reading metadata blocks backward from [=archive/metadata_backward_offset=]
  until the path or one of its ancestors is found as a file in a segment.
</div>

<!-- Obsolete drafts of the compressor, the decompressor and transformers follow, superseded by the declarative semantics above. -->

<!--
<p>To <dfn export>compress a file</dfn> into an Aqueous archive, given |inputPath| (a file system path), |outputPath| (a file system path), and optional |options|:
</p>

<ol class=algorithm>
  <li>Let |inputFile| be the result of opening |inputPath| for reading.
    If |inputFile| is null, [=throw=] an error.</li>

  <li>Let |inputSize| be the size of |inputFile| in bytes.</li>

  <li>Let |outputFile| be the result of opening |outputPath| for writing.
    If |outputFile| is null, [=throw=] an error.</li>

  <li>Let |metadataBlocks| be an empty list.</li>
  <li>Let |contentBlocks| be an empty list.</li>
  <li>Let |transformerBlocks| be an empty list.</li>
  <li>Let |dictionaryBlocks| be an empty list.</li>

  <li>Let |transformerIndex| be 0.</li>
  <li>Let |dictionaryIndex| be 0.</li>

  <li>Write the [=archive/header_magic=] to |outputFile|.</li>

  <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["size_hint"] is true:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |sizeHintBlock| be a [=size hint block=] with [=szhint/size_hint=] set to 0 (placeholder).</li>
      <li>Write |sizeHintBlock| to |outputFile|.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["transformer"] is provided:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |transformerData| be |options|["transformer"].</li>
      <li>Let |compressedTransformer| be the result of [=compressing a byte sequence=] with |transformerData| using the Zstandard compression method.
        If the compression fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
      <li>Let |transformerBlock| be a [=transformer block=] containing |compressedTransformer|.</li>
      <li>Append |transformerBlock| to |transformerBlocks|.</li>
      <li>Increment |transformerIndex| by 1.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["dictionary"] is provided:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |dictionaryData| be |options|["dictionary"].</li>
      <li>Let |compressedDictionary| be the result of [=compressing a byte sequence=] with |dictionaryData| using the Zstandard compression method.
        If the compression fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
      <li>Let |dictionaryBlock| be a [=dictionary block=] containing |compressedDictionary|.</li>
      <li>Append |dictionaryBlock| to |dictionaryBlocks|.</li>
      <li>Increment |dictionaryIndex| by 1.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>Let |chunkSize| be the maximum chunk size.
    If |options| is provided and |options|["chunk_size"] is provided, set |chunkSize| to |options|["chunk_size"].
    Otherwise, set |chunkSize| to 2<sup>21</sup> - 1 (the maximum Zstandard block size).</li>

  <li>Let |chunkOffset| be 0.</li>
  <li>Let |chunkIndex| be 0.</li>
  <li>Let |compressedChunks| be an empty list.</li>

  <li>While |chunkOffset| is less than |inputSize|:
    <ol>
      <li>Let |remainingSize| be |inputSize| - |chunkOffset|.</li>
      <li>Let |currentChunkSize| be min(|remainingSize|, |chunkSize|).</li>

      <li>Let |chunkData| be the result of reading |currentChunkSize| bytes from |inputFile| starting at offset |chunkOffset|.
        If the read fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>

      <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["transformer"] is provided:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |transformedData| be the result of applying the transformer specified by |options|["transformer"] to |chunkData| as preprocessing.
            If the transformation fails, [=throw=] an error.</li>
          <li>Set |chunkData| to |transformedData|.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Let |compressionParams| be an empty byte sequence.</li>
      <li>Let |compressedData| be an empty byte sequence.
      <li>Let |useCompression| be true.</li>

      <li>If |options| is provided and |options|["dictionary"] is provided:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |dictionary| be the dictionary data from |dictionaryBlocks|[0].</li>
          <li>Set |compressionParams| to the Zstandard [=window_descriptor=] for |dictionary|.</li>
          <li>Let |compressedData| be the result of compressing |chunkData| using Zstandard with |dictionary|.
            If the compression fails, set |useCompression| to false.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
      <li>Otherwise:
        <ol>
          <li>Let |compressedData| be the result of compressing |chunkData| using Zstandard without a dictionary.
            If the compression fails, set |useCompression| to false.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>

      <li>Determine whether to use compression:
        <ol>
          <li>If |useCompression| is false, continue to the next step.</li>

          <li>Let |compressedSize| be the length of |compressedData| in bytes.</li>
          <li>Let |originalSize| be the length of |chunkData| in bytes.</li>

          <li>If |compressedSize| is greater than or equal to |originalSize|:
            <ol>
              <li>Set |useCompression| to false.
              <div class=note>
                This indicates that the data is [=barely compressible=] and should be stored uncompressed.
                For Zstandard, the minimum overhead is 3 bytes per block, so if the size saving is less than 3 bytes,
                compression should not be used.
